## Normalizer

`normalize` is depth-first (children transformed before the collection
itself, the same traversal shape as `yin.vm/strip-reader-positions`, cited
in the docstring and reused for a different rewrite). Per node, `normalize-
node` handles three design-stated rules and defers everything else to
`yin.vm.parity-test/normalize`:

- `{:type :closure ...}` -> `{:type :closure, :arity n}` (arity from
  `:params`, or passed through unchanged if `:arity` is already present, so
  a second normalization pass is a no-op)
- `{:type :cursor-ref ...}` -> `{:type :cursor-ref, :id id}`
- `{:type :parked-continuation}` / `{:type :reified-continuation}` -> bare
  `{:type ...}`, dropping `:id`, `:k`, `:env`
- everything else -> `yin.vm.parity-test/normalize`, which already handles
  `{:type :stream-ref, :id id}` (by identity) and `fn? -> :host-fn`; this is
  the "reuse, don't reinvent" part the task asked for.

`normalize-error` is `{:message (ex-message ex), :data (normalize (ex-data
ex))}` per the design's stated error rule.

Host handles and telemetry: the default VM's store carries `:yin/call-in`,
`:yin/call-out`, `:yin/call-out-cursor` (live ring-buffer handles + a
per-run UUID cursor identity) even for a program that never touches
streams. These are not `:type`-tagged values `normalize-node` can catch
generically, so `store-normalization-test` dissocs them before normalizing,
reusing `yin.vm.completion/ffi-pair-keys` -- the same set
`completion-test.cljc` already excludes from a store slice (`(not-any?
completion/ffi-pair-keys (keys (:yin.k/store result)))`) -- rather than
inventing a second name for the same three keys. This was the one point
where the plain per-node cond was not enough; first run of the store test
failed on non-deterministic RingHandle/UUID content before this fix.

## Corpus

`tag-corpus` = `yin.vm-test/semantic-bytecode-corpus` (content_test's own
reuse target, unchanged, required directly). Combined with
`yin.vm.parity-test/corpus` for the coverage assertion and for named-VM
self-parity.

Deviation: `yin.vm.completion-test`'s own extra fixtures (`scoping-corpus`,
`kitchen-sink`, `maker-program`, and `conformance-corpus` itself) are all
`^:private`, and the file box forbids editing that namespace to expose
them. They are not required into this namespace. This does not weaken the
coverage claim: `yin.vm-test/semantic-bytecode-corpus-covers-every-tag`
(existing test, unedited) already proves `tag-corpus` alone spans every key
of `semantic-bytecode-grammar` (16 tags: :literal :variable :lambda
:application :if :dao.stream.apply/call :vm/gensym :vm/store-get
:vm/store-put :vm/current-continuation :vm/park :vm/resume :stream/make
:stream/put :stream/cursor :stream/next :stream/close), so this
namespace's own `all-node-fixture-coverage-test` (re-deriving the same set
from `tag-corpus` + `parity/corpus`, same technique as vm_test.cljc's own
coverage test) is provably not weaker than reusing completion_test's
private extras would have been.

## Completion criteria -> deftest

- Named-VM self-parity: `named-vm-self-parity-test` -- every
  `parity/corpus` program, two independent named-VM runs each, normalized
  results `=`; plus, for the same corpus, the datom-batch path (`v2/eval`)
  vs the row-load path (`ast-walker/vm-load-rows` + `v2/run`), the same two
  paths `parity-test/ast-walker-parity-test` already compares.
- Idempotence: `normalizer-idempotence-test` -- 13 fixtures (scalars,
  collections, closure, stream-ref, cursor-ref, parked-continuation,
  reified-continuation, a host fn, a nested store-shaped map) each checked
  `(normalize x) = (normalize (normalize x))`.
- Closures: `closure-normalization-test`.
- Continuations (UCF-shaped): `continuation-normalization-test`, both
  `:vm/current-continuation` (reified) and `:vm/park` (parked) sub-cases.
- Errors: `error-normalization-test`, applying a literal as a function
  (`ast_walker.cljc`'s "Cannot apply non-function" throw site) and
  normalizing the caught exception.
- Streams: `stream-normalization-test`.
- Cursors: `cursor-normalization-test`.
- Store snapshots: `store-normalization-test`.
- Duplicate parameters: `duplicate-parameter-normalization-test` -- a
  lambda with params `[x x]` applied to `[1 2]`; asserts the named VM's
  actual rightmost-wins behaviour (`engine/bind-params` zips params over
  args into a map via `into {}`, so a repeated name keeps its last binding)
  yields `2`, that this is self-parity-stable, and that the closure's own
  arity (unapplied) is `2` -- arity counts declared params, not distinct
  names.
- All-node coverage: `all-node-fixture-coverage-test`.

